/**
 * \page page_config Configuration reference
 *
 * Every key MinFlyout understands, what it does, what it defaults to and what
 * happens at the edges of its range. \ref config_examples follows with complete
 * files for a small laptop, two screens of different shape, a tablet that turns,
 * and a session spent chasing an app that stays undetected.
 *
 * The mechanisms behind the settings are described on the main page:
 * \ref main_capture writes a zone down without counting pixels,
 * \ref main_monitor_layouts explains the monitor selectors,
 * \ref main_detection what \c "buttonDetection" chooses between, and
 * \ref main_diagnosis what to do when a window stays undetected.
 *
 * \section config_file The file
 *
 * On first start \c \%APPDATA%\\MinFlyout\\config.jsonc is created with a
 * commented template - the same text \ref mfly::ConfigStore::Reload writes, so
 * the template and the defaults documented here cannot drift apart.
 *
 * The format is JSONC, read by the parser in \ref Json.h: line comments
 * (<code>//</code>), block comments and trailing commas are allowed.
 *
 * \c res/config.schema.json describes the whole file, so an editor can complete
 * the keys, show these descriptions on hover and flag a typo such as
 * \c showAllMonitor. In VS Code, either add an entry to \c json.schemas in the
 * **user** settings - appending to the array rather than replacing it - with
 * <code>fileMatch</code> set to <code>&#42;&#42;&#47;MinFlyout/config.jsonc</code>
 * and <code>url</code> to the full path of \c res/config.schema.json; or put
 * <code>"$schema": "d:/Projects/MinFlyout/res/config.schema.json"</code> as the
 * first key of the file itself. MinFlyout ignores keys it does not know, so the
 * file keeps working either way. Forward slashes are accepted in that path and
 * save the escaping.
 *
 * The trailing commas of the template stay flagged with a warning: the JSONC
 * mode accepts them, but VS Code marks them anyway and offers no setting for
 * that single diagnostic - the only switch is \c "json.validate.enable": \c false,
 * which would also disable the schema.
 *
 * **Saving applies the changes.** \ref mfly::ConfigWatcher watches the folder,
 * debounces for 300 ms and posts one message per save; a balloon on the tray
 * icon confirms the reload. The tray menu entry *Reload configuration* does the
 * same by hand, and \c "watchConfig": \c false turns the watching off.
 *
 * \section config_top Top-level keys
 *
 * | Key | Type | Default | Meaning |
 * |---|---|---|---|
 * | \c hoverDelayMs | integer, 0 - 5000 | \c 350 | How long the pointer must rest on the minimize button before the flyout opens. \c 0 opens on the first movement over it, which makes crossing the title bar noisy. (\ref mfly::Config::hoverDelayMs) |
 * | \c closeGraceMs | integer, 0 - 5000 | \c 260 | How long the flyout stays after the pointer has left it and the button. It survives the trip between the two regardless - a corridor spanning both is treated as inside. (\ref mfly::Config::closeGraceMs) |
 * | \c showBuiltinItems | boolean | \c true | Show the text items below the miniatures. \c false leaves the layouts alone. (\ref mfly::Config::showBuiltinItems) |
 * | \c useWorkArea | boolean | \c true | \c true bases every percentage on the work area, so a zone of 100 % height stops above the taskbar. \c false uses the full monitor area. Applies to the flyout, to the touch overlay and to what Ctrl+Alt+F11 measures, so the three always mean the same thing. (\ref mfly::Config::useWorkArea) |
 * | \c showAllMonitors | boolean | \c true | \c true draws one row per screen, so one click can move a window to another monitor. \c false shows only the screen the window is on. (\ref mfly::Config::showAllMonitors) |
 * | \c uiScale | number, 0.75 - 3 | \c 1.0 | Size factor of the flyout, on top of the DPI scaling; the details are in \ref config_uiscale below. (\ref mfly::Config::uiScale) |
 * | \c buttonDetection | \c "auto", \c "hittest", \c "computed" | \c "auto" | How the minimize button is located; \ref main_detection walks the three. An unknown value is a configuration error and leaves the default in place. (\ref mfly::Config::buttonDetection) |
 * | \c traceDetection | boolean | \c false | One log line per gate of the detection, so a mouse movement that never reaches the probe can be followed to where it died. One line per change, not per movement, and only in a build with the logging macros compiled in. (\ref mfly::Config::traceDetection) |
 * | \c logToFile | boolean | \c false | Also write the log to \c \%APPDATA%\\MinFlyout\\minflyout.log, UTF-8 with BOM, rotated to \c .log.1 at 1 MB. The debugger output is always written; this only adds the file, and only where logging is compiled in. (\ref mfly::Config::logToFile) |
 * | \c watchConfig | boolean | \c true | Reload as soon as the file is saved. Worth turning off if \c \%APPDATA% is redirected to a share that reports no changes. (\ref mfly::Config::watchConfig) |
 * | \c layouts | array of \ref config_layout | five built-in | The layouts the flyout offers. A missing key falls back to \ref mfly::DefaultLayouts; an explicitly empty list \c [] hides the miniature area and leaves only the text items. (\ref mfly::Config::layouts) |
 * | \c touch | object | see \ref config_touch | Dragging a window into a zone with the finger. (\ref mfly::Config::touch) |
 *
 * Numbers outside their range are clamped rather than rejected: a
 * \c hoverDelayMs of 100000 becomes 5000 and the file still loads. The only
 * values that make the load fail are structural - a \c "layouts" that is not an
 * array, a \c "buttonDetection" nobody knows, a malformed \c "touch".
 * \ref config_errors says what happens then.
 *
 * \section config_uiscale Making the flyout bigger
 *
 * Windows sizes the flyout for the resolution of the screen, which on a small
 * panel means small - correct in millimetres, and awkward to hit.
 * \c "uiScale" multiplies that.
 *
 * \code{.js}
 *   "uiScale": 1.4,
 * \endcode
 *
 * The factor is folded into the DPI the flyout is laid out for
 * (\ref mfly::ScaledDpi), and every metric already goes through that DPI. Fonts,
 * paddings, corner radii and the miniatures therefore grow together and nothing
 * can be pulled out of proportion - it is the same multiplication a
 * higher-resolution screen would apply. Accepted range is
 * \ref mfly::kMinUiScale to \ref mfly::kMaxUiScale; values below 1 shrink it.
 *
 * A factor is a wish, not an instruction. On the very screen it is meant for it
 * may ask for more room than there is, so \ref mfly::FlyoutWindow::Show compares
 * the measured size with the work area and walks the factor back until it fits -
 * but never below 1.0, so asking for a larger flyout can never hand out a
 * smaller one. A reduction is written to the log.
 *
 * The touch overlay has no such setting: its trigger field and its zones are
 * given in percent of the screen and are therefore already as large as the
 * screen is.
 *
 * \section config_zone The zone object
 *
 * A \ref mfly::Zone is a rectangle in percent of the reference area - the work
 * area or the full monitor, depending on \c "useWorkArea".
 *
 * | Field | Range | Default | Meaning |
 * |---|---|---|---|
 * | \c left   | 0 - 99  | \c 0   | Left edge in percent. |
 * | \c top    | 0 - 99  | \c 0   | Top edge in percent. |
 * | \c width  | 1 - 100 | \c 50  | Width in percent. |
 * | \c height | 1 - 100 | \c 100 | Height in percent. |
 *
 * The ranges are exactly the ones \ref mfly::ZoneFromWindow clamps a measured
 * zone to, so a line produced by Ctrl+Alt+F11 comes back out of the file as the
 * window it was taken from.
 *
 * Zones are meant to tile the area without overlapping, the way the Windows snap
 * layouts do - but nothing enforces it. Where they do overlap, the smallest one
 * under the pointer wins (\ref mfly::SmallestHit), so a small tile drawn on top
 * of a large one stays reachable. Fractions are allowed and are how thirds are
 * spelled: \c 33.33 / \c 33.34 / \c 66.66.
 *
 * \section config_layout The layout object
 *
 * | Field | Type | Required | Meaning |
 * |---|---|---|---|
 * | \c name | string | no | Caption below the miniature, and the label in the touch trigger field. |
 * | \c zones | array of \ref config_zone | yes | The tiles, in drawing order. A layout without a single usable zone is dropped silently. |
 * | \c monitors | selector or array of selectors | no | Which screens the layout is offered on; omitted means all of them. The accepted forms are listed in \ref main_monitor_layouts on the main page. |
 *
 * \section config_touch The touch block
 *
 * Pick a window up by its title bar with the finger, rest it in the field that
 * appears, and the zones unfold across the screen; let go over one of them and
 * the window lands there. \ref mfly::DragWatcher notices the drag,
 * \ref mfly::ZoneOverlay draws the target.
 *
 * | Key | Type | Default | Meaning |
 * |---|---|---|---|
 * | \c enabled | boolean | \c true | Whether the drag route is offered at all. (\ref mfly::TouchConfig::enabled) |
 * | \c alsoMouse | boolean | \c false | React to a window dragged with the mouse as well. Off by default - with a mouse the flyout is the shorter way - and the way to try the overlay out on a machine without a touchscreen. (\ref mfly::TouchConfig::alsoMouse) |
 * | \c dwellMs | integer, 0 - 5000 | \c 250 | How long the finger must rest in the trigger field before the zones unfold. Too short and merely carrying a window across the middle of the screen opens them. (\ref mfly::TouchConfig::dwellMs) |
 * | \c trigger | \ref config_zone | 40 / 40 / 20 / 20 | Where the field sits, in percent of the same reference area the zones use. It only exists while a window is actually being dragged, so it may be generous. (\ref mfly::TouchConfig::trigger) |
 * | \c layouts | array of \ref config_layout | the flyout's | The zone sets offered while dragging. (\ref mfly::TouchConfig::layouts) |
 *
 * Touch has its own layouts because it is a different task. Picking one of five
 * miniatures with a mouse is a menu; doing it with a window already in mid-drag
 * is not. So there is exactly **one** drop target: the first layout in
 * \c touch.layouts whose \c "monitors" selector matches the screen the finger is
 * on (\ref mfly::LayoutsForMonitor). Leave \c layouts out entirely and the
 * flyout's layouts are used instead, so a configuration that never heard of
 * touch gains the drag route without being edited.
 *
 * A screen no layout applies to simply shows no field there; the drag stays an
 * ordinary drag, and moving to a screen that has one still works.
 *
 * \section config_errors When the file does not parse
 *
 * The application keeps running with the default values and reports line and
 * column of the error - in the flyout as a clickable item, and on reload as a
 * balloon on the tray icon (\ref mfly::Config::error). Nothing is silently half
 * applied: either a load succeeds and replaces the configuration, or the
 * previous values stay in place.
 *
 * Individual entries are more forgiving than the file as a whole. A layout
 * without a usable zone, a zone that is not an object, a monitor selector that
 * is neither a number nor a string - each is dropped and the rest is kept,
 * because one malformed layout is not worth refusing a whole file over.
 *
 * \section config_examples Examples
 *
 * \subsection config_ex_laptop A small laptop screen
 *
 * The flyout enlarged, and only the two arrangements that are comfortable on a
 * 13-inch panel. Thirds are deliberately absent - they are cramped there.
 *
 * \code{.js}
 * {
 *   "uiScale": 1.4,
 *   "hoverDelayMs": 300,
 *
 *   "layouts": [
 *     {
 *       "name": "Halves",
 *       "zones": [
 *         { "left":  0, "top": 0, "width": 50, "height": 100 },
 *         { "left": 50, "top": 0, "width": 50, "height": 100 },
 *       ],
 *     },
 *     {
 *       "name": "Quarters",
 *       "zones": [
 *         { "left":  0, "top":  0, "width": 50, "height": 50 },
 *         { "left": 50, "top":  0, "width": 50, "height": 50 },
 *         { "left":  0, "top": 50, "width": 50, "height": 50 },
 *         { "left": 50, "top": 50, "width": 50, "height": 50 },
 *       ],
 *     },
 *   ],
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * \subsection config_ex_two_screens An ultrawide next to a laptop
 *
 * Thirds belong on the wide screen, halves on the laptop panel, and the full
 * screen everywhere. Every layout names the screens it is offered on, so each
 * row of the flyout carries only what makes sense there.
 *
 * \code{.js}
 * {
 *   "showAllMonitors": true,
 *
 *   "layouts": [
 *     {
 *       "name": "Thirds",
 *       "monitors": [ "3840x2160" ],
 *       "zones": [
 *         { "left":  0,    "top": 0, "width": 33.34, "height": 100 },
 *         { "left": 33.33, "top": 0, "width": 33.34, "height": 100 },
 *         { "left": 66.66, "top": 0, "width": 33.34, "height": 100 },
 *       ],
 *     },
 *     {
 *       "name": "Wide + two",
 *       "monitors": [ "3840x2160" ],
 *       "zones": [
 *         { "left":  0, "top":  0, "width": 66, "height": 100 },
 *         { "left": 66, "top":  0, "width": 34, "height":  50 },
 *         { "left": 66, "top": 50, "width": 34, "height":  50 },
 *       ],
 *     },
 *     {
 *       "name": "Halves",
 *       "monitors": [ "secondary" ],
 *       "zones": [
 *         { "left":  0, "top": 0, "width": 50, "height": 100 },
 *         { "left": 50, "top": 0, "width": 50, "height": 100 },
 *       ],
 *     },
 *     {
 *       "name": "Full screen",
 *       "zones": [
 *         { "left": 0, "top": 0, "width": 100, "height": 100 },
 *       ],
 *     },
 *   ],
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * The selectors may be mixed in one list - <code>[1, "secondary"]</code> is
 * valid - and a screen that no layout applies to is left out of the flyout
 * entirely. That is also the way to exclude one: give every layout a list that
 * omits it.
 *
 * \subsection config_ex_tablet A tablet that turns
 *
 * Rotating a Surface changes its resolution, so the resolution selector is also
 * an orientation selector. Two touch layouts, one per posture: side by side when
 * the screen is wide, stacked when it is tall.
 *
 * \code{.js}
 * {
 *   "uiScale": 1.5,
 *
 *   "touch": {
 *     "enabled": true,
 *     "dwellMs": 250,
 *     "trigger": { "left": 38, "top": 38, "width": 24, "height": 24 },
 *
 *     "layouts": [
 *       {
 *         "name": "Landscape",
 *         "monitors": [ "2880x1920" ],
 *         "zones": [
 *           { "left":  0, "top":  0, "width": 50, "height": 100 },
 *           { "left": 50, "top":  0, "width": 50, "height":  50 },
 *           { "left": 50, "top": 50, "width": 50, "height":  50 },
 *         ],
 *       },
 *       {
 *         "name": "Portrait",
 *         "monitors": [ "1920x2880" ],
 *         "zones": [
 *           { "left": 0, "top":  0, "width": 100, "height": 50 },
 *           { "left": 0, "top": 50, "width": 100, "height": 50 },
 *         ],
 *       },
 *     ],
 *   },
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * The first matching layout is the one that shows, so the two lists must not
 * both match the same screen - which is what the two resolutions guarantee.
 *
 * \subsection config_ex_touch_mouse Trying the touch route without a touchscreen
 *
 * \c "alsoMouse" makes the overlay react to a window dragged with the mouse, so
 * the whole route can be developed and watched at a desk. Everything else stays
 * as it is.
 *
 * \code{.js}
 * {
 *   "touch": {
 *     "alsoMouse": true,
 *     "dwellMs": 400,
 *   },
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * A longer dwell helps here: a mouse crosses the middle of the screen faster and
 * more often than a finger does.
 *
 * \subsection config_ex_items Text items only
 *
 * An explicitly empty layout list is a valid setting, and a different one from a
 * missing key: it hides the miniature area instead of falling back to the
 * built-in layouts. What remains is minimize, minimize to the notification area,
 * always on top, minimize all windows of this app, minimize other windows and
 * show desktop.
 *
 * \code{.js}
 * {
 *   "layouts": [],
 *   "touch": { "enabled": false },
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * \subsection config_ex_debug Chasing an app that stays undetected
 *
 * Nothing here changes what the flyout offers - it changes what MinFlyout says
 * about itself. \ref main_diagnosis explains how to read the result.
 *
 * \code{.js}
 * {
 *   // Log every gate of the detection, not just the successful ones.
 *   "traceDetection": true,
 *
 *   // ... and keep it, so the log survives the next start.
 *   "logToFile": true,
 *
 *   // Never ask the window, always compute - which shows whether the app
 *   // answers WM_NCHITTEST at all or whether the geometry is what is wrong.
 *   "buttonDetection": "computed",
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * Both log settings only take effect in a build with the logging macros compiled
 * in (\c _DEBUG or \c _RELEASE_WITH_DEBUG_LOG). The Ctrl+Alt+F12 diagnosis works
 * in a plain release build regardless, and says on its second line which of the
 * two is running.
 */
